From 57bbe5092dd7bb3b9c74abcc9e718da2e2703faa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:29:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(update): one absolute 30s deadline bounds the opted-in
 pre-swap stop [daveey]

The opted-in busy stop started its 30 s clock after the serial drain requests and then ran
blocking supervisor stops (120 s client timeout plus the unit-job wait, up to 900 s) and
10 s bare waits past it, so a hung stop could hold the update far beyond the bound.

_pause now starts one deadline before discovery; the drain RPCs, the wait, supervisor stops and
the verification all spend it. Supervisor stops are queued (systemctl stop --no-block, a
background launchctl bootout, SIGTERM for bare) and polled; survivors are killed through the
supervisor (systemctl kill -s SIGKILL / SIGKILL) before the deadline. Each gateway must be
verified stopped (process gone, unit inactive with no job, launchd job without a pid) before
the checkout may move; otherwise the pause refuses and the existing durable record rolls back.
Unopted callers keep the idle-only path.
